Source package: glib2.0

scope-before-exec.patch
    Puts each application which GDesktopAppInfo launches into its own systemd scope before the
    application starts, app-gnome-<application ID>-<pid>.scope, so that every process of the
    application starts in that scope. GNOME Shell, gnome-session and gnome-settings-daemon asked
    systemd for the scope only once GLib had started the application, and systemd moves only the
    process it is given, so whatever the application started in the meantime stayed in the cgroup
    of the service which launched it, and was stopped with that service. Signal's zygotes and GPU
    process stayed in gnome-session-manager@ubuntu.service, and Thunderbird's crash helper in
    org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service.

    GLib sends the request right after it spawned gio-launch-desktop, before the launch call
    returns, and gio-launch-desktop, which executes the application, first waits until
    /proc/self/cgroup names the scope. The wait does not depend on the launching process once the
    request is sent, so it works for a process which exits right after launching too, such as
    gio open. A pipe from the launching process tells gio-launch-desktop when systemd refused to
    create the scope, and gio-launch-desktop waits 5 seconds at most, for when the scope is never
    created and nothing could tell. That limit is reached only by a process which launches an
    application on a session bus without systemd, as a test's dbus-run-session, and exits before
    the refusal arrives. Starting an application waits about 25 ms longer for the scope, and the
    launch call itself returns about 3 ms later, as it waits until the request is sent. A launching
    process which never runs its main loop again keeps the write end of each pipe open, as only
    the reply to the request closes it.

    Processes started without GDesktopAppInfo are not covered. GNOME Shell spawns a command run
    with Alt+F2 itself, in trySpawn in js/misc/util.js, and asks for its scope afterwards, and
    gnome-settings-daemon does the same for the fallback Orca.

    The scope is requested only by a process managed by the systemd user instance, and not in a
    Flatpak or a snap. The name is the one gnome-desktop's gnome_start_systemd_scope() gives the
    scope, so gnome-session's /usr/lib/systemd/user/app-gnome-.scope.d/ applies, and a launching
    process which still asks for the scope afterwards fails without moving anything, while
    gnome_start_systemd_scope() logs a warning about it. The "launched" signal names the scope as
    "systemd-unit" in its platform data, which the patches of the same name in patches/gnome-shell/,
    patches/gnome-session/ and patches/gnome-settings-daemon/ look for to leave the scope to GLib.
    A snap's command, snap run, moves itself into a scope of its own before it executes the snap,
    which is after GLib's scope, so the snap still ends up in its own.

    The approach follows https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/1596, without its
    shell wrapper, as gio-launch-desktop is back in the launch since GLib 2.86. GLib's developers
    prepared GDesktopAppInfo for scopes in https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/5350
    (merged after 2.88.0), with the scopes themselves announced as a follow-up. Drop this patch,
    and the three which depend on it, once GLib creates scopes itself, and check then which name and
    which platform data key it uses.

    The request is sent in g_desktop_app_info_launch_uris_with_spawn in gio/gdesktopappinfo.c, and
    the wait is wait_for_scope in gio/gio-launch-desktop.c. After a version bump, check that
    launching still spawns gio-launch-desktop and emits "launched" only after the spawn.

    The patch adds two tests to gio/tests/desktop-app-info.c, launch-desktop/scope-refused and
    launch-desktop/scope-pending, for the wait of gio-launch-desktop. The build runs outside of a
    systemd user instance, so the scope itself is checked by tests/run-scope-test.sh, in a
    container with a user instance of its own, see tests/README.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      systemd-cgls --user-unit gnome-session-manager@ubuntu.service
                                  should list gnome-session-service alone
      systemd-cgls --user-unit org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service
                                  should list gnome-shell, Xwayland and mutter-x11-frames alone,
                                  also after starting Thunderbird or Signal from the dock
      journalctl --user -b | grep 'Could not create transient scope'
                                  should find nothing
